{"version":3,"kind":"Article","sha256":"a996d0f08bfd8b8e82ed1506f344a76d03b6f216faf85e3576a21cbdda9c1076","slug":"chapters.16.inference-for-regression","location":"/chapters/16/Inference_for_Regression.md","dependencies":[],"frontmatter":{"title":"Inference for Regression","content_includes_title":false,"authors":[{"nameParsed":{"literal":"Ani Adhikari","given":"Ani","family":"Adhikari"},"name":"Ani Adhikari","id":"contributors-myst-generated-uid-0"},{"nameParsed":{"literal":"John DeNero","given":"John","family":"DeNero"},"name":"John DeNero","id":"contributors-myst-generated-uid-1"},{"nameParsed":{"literal":"David Wagner","given":"David","family":"Wagner"},"name":"David Wagner","id":"contributors-myst-generated-uid-2"}],"github":"https://github.com/data-8/textbook","numbering":{"title":{"enabled":true},"heading_1":{"enabled":true},"heading_2":{"enabled":true},"heading_3":{"enabled":true},"heading_4":{"enabled":true},"heading_5":{"enabled":true},"heading_6":{"enabled":true}},"source_url":"https://github.com/data-8/textbook/blob/main/chapters/16/Inference_for_Regression.md","edit_url":"https://github.com/data-8/textbook/edit/main/chapters/16/Inference_for_Regression.md","enumerator":"16","exports":[{"format":"md","filename":"Inference_for_Regression.md","url":"/build/Inference_for_Regres-2da462bb2a0c19f1ae29b693ec5b43ed.md"}]},"mdast":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"block","children":[{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":3,"column":1},"end":{"line":3,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Thus far, our analysis of the relation between variables has been purely descriptive. We know how to find the best straight line to draw through a scatter plot. The line is the best in the sense that it has the smallest mean squared error of estimation among all straight lines.","position":{"start":{"line":3,"column":1},"end":{"line":3,"column":1}},"key":"sYQmw30QIW"}],"key":"nCm9gSvRNF"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":5,"column":1},"end":{"line":5,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"But what if our data were only a sample from a larger population? If in the sample we found a linear relation between the two variables, would the same be true for the population? Would it be exactly the same linear relation? Could we predict the response of a new individual who is not in our sample?","position":{"start":{"line":5,"column":1},"end":{"line":5,"column":1}},"key":"E08mvJoP5e"}],"key":"mbvyuYa6GF"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":7,"column":1},"end":{"line":7,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Such questions of inference and prediction arise if we believe that a scatter plot reflects the underlying relation between the two variables being plotted but does not specify the relation completely. For example, a scatter plot of birth weight versus gestational days shows us the precise relation between the two variables in our sample; but we might wonder whether that relation holds true, or almost true, for all babies in the population from which the sample was drawn, or indeed among babies in general.","position":{"start":{"line":7,"column":1},"end":{"line":7,"column":1}},"key":"FA6GnfnnsS"}],"key":"VXAzPzHShx"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":9,"column":1},"end":{"line":9,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"As always, inferential thinking begins with a careful examination of the assumptions about the data. Sets of assumptions are known as ","position":{"start":{"line":9,"column":1},"end":{"line":9,"column":1}},"key":"eiFbW8CcMQ"},{"type":"emphasis","position":{"start":{"line":9,"column":1},"end":{"line":9,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"models","position":{"start":{"line":9,"column":1},"end":{"line":9,"column":1}},"key":"x2h57icv0R"}],"key":"O9pDY4XI8z"},{"type":"text","value":". Sets of assumptions about randomness in roughly linear scatter plots are called ","position":{"start":{"line":9,"column":1},"end":{"line":9,"column":1}},"key":"OggwqWhnrT"},{"type":"emphasis","position":{"start":{"line":9,"column":1},"end":{"line":9,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"regression models","position":{"start":{"line":9,"column":1},"end":{"line":9,"column":1}},"key":"C576ekgBmc"}],"key":"Y830c5fw8z"},{"type":"text","value":".","position":{"start":{"line":9,"column":1},"end":{"line":9,"column":1}},"key":"jY20fsokwv"}],"key":"IkDlrcZkDU"}],"key":"LeO9lRPZ37"}],"key":"jqo8aPOOuz"},"references":{"cite":{"order":[],"data":{}}},"footer":{"navigation":{"prev":{"title":"Numerical Diagnostics","url":"/chapters/15/6/numerical-diagnostics","group":"Computational and Inferential Thinking"},"next":{"title":"A Regression Model","url":"/chapters/16/1/regression-model","group":"Computational and Inferential Thinking"}}},"domain":"http://localhost:3000"}